<speak>
<p>A real circulant Hadamard matrix of positive integer order n exists if and only if n is one or four. We finish both directions of this statement: exclude every other order, and verify that the two remaining orders occur.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Suppose first that n is greater than one. The order restriction from Node Two gives n equal to four u squared, where u is a positive odd integer. The general contradiction excludes every u greater than one. Thus u equals one, and n equals four.<break time="700ms"/>The order-one case is outside the hypothesis n greater than one of that restriction. Keep it on a separate path, and check its existence directly.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>At order one, take the one-by-one matrix with entry one. Its entry is a sign, and its only row has squared length one. There are no distinct rows to compare. A cyclic shift fixes this single entry.<break time="700ms"/>Multiplying the matrix by its transpose gives the one-by-one identity. This verifies the order-one example.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>At order four, take the matrix whose first row is negative one, one, one, one. Each following row is the cyclic right shift of the preceding row. This moves the negative sign through all four positions.<break time="700ms"/>Any two distinct rows agree in two positions and disagree in two. Their dot product is therefore two positive ones plus two negative ones, which is zero. Each row has squared length four. So the matrix times its transpose has fours on the diagonal and zeros elsewhere: H times H transpose equals four times the identity.<break time="700ms"/>Both remaining orders occur. The order-four example has u equal to one, outside the earlier Delta theorem's hypothesis u greater than one, so it is consistent with the contradiction. This completes both directions of the proof chain, using the stated inputs developed in the earlier nodes.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
